{:edb/db-before-t 2549 :edb/db-after-t 2550 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535862 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:16:38.648Z" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128785 1100 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and center of mass $(0,1)$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $2.5\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{m}^2$, what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(2,1)$?" 13194139535862 false] [17592186128785 1100 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and center of mass $(0,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $2.5\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{m}^2,$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(2,1)?$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128785 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(2 - 0)^{2} + (1 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{l} &= 2.5 + 2^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2.5 + 8 \\\\\n&= 10.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}$." 13194139535862 false] [17592186128785 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-0)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =2.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}.$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128786 1100 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2)$, measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2$, about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139535862 false] [17592186128786 1100 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2),$ measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139535862 true] [17592186128786 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y$, $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0)$. The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2\n$$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}2r^{2} \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2r^{3}dr \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2π}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{4} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\cdot θ \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2π \\\\\n&= πkg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{M} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}2r^{2} \\cdot r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2r^{5}dr \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2π}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}r^{6} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\cdot θ \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2π \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= I_{M} + d^{2}M \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3} + 1^{2} \\cdot π \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3} + π \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 false] [17592186128786 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi \\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128799 1100 "A circular plate of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $4 \\, \\textrm{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $24 \\, \\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}^2$, find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D$." 13194139535862 false] [17592186128799 1100 "A circular plate of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $4 \\, \\textrm{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $24 \\, \\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}^2,$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128799 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi \\cdot 4^2 = 16\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2}{16\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{8\\pi}\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0)$.\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{M} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8π}∫_{0}^{4}r^{3}dr∫_{0}^{2π}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8π} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}r^{4} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\cdot θ \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8π} \\cdot 64 \\cdot 2π \\\\\n&= 16kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= I_{M} + d^{2}M \\\\\n24 &= 16 + 2d^{2} \\\\\n2d^{2} &= 8 \\\\\nd^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nd &= 2m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 false] [17592186128799 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = \\pi \\cdot 4^2 = 16\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2}{16\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{8\\pi}\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 24 & =16+2{d}^{2} \\\\ 2{d}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ d & =2\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186128800 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le 2, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 4 \\big\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 true] [17592186128800 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le 2, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 4 \\big\\}$,\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 false] [17592186128800 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{3}8\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{0+2}2, \\dfrac{0+4}2 \\right) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{M} &= ∬_(D)((x - x^(¯))^{2} + (y - y^(¯))^{2})λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{8}((x - 1)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2})dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{4}3(x - 1)^{2} + 3(y - 2)^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∫_{0}^{2}[3(x - 1)^{2}y + (y - 2)^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∫_{0}^{2}12(x - 1)^{2} + 16dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}[4(x - 1)^{3} + 16x]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}(4 + 32 - (-4 + 0)) \\\\\n&= 5kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= I_{M} + d^{2}M \\\\\n&= 5 + 1^{2} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 5 + 3 \\\\\n&= 8kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 false] [17592186128800 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{3}8\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right) = \\left( \\dfrac{0+2}2, \\dfrac{0+4}2 \\right) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{8}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-2)^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12(x-1)^{2}+16\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[4(x-1)^{3}+16x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4+32-(-4+0)) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+3 \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186129208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371016 13194139535862 true] [17592186129208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371023 13194139535862 true] [17592186129208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371030 13194139535862 true] [17592186129208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371037 13194139535862 true] [17592186129208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371044 13194139535862 true] [17592186129209 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371002 13194139535862 true] [17592186129209 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371009 13194139535862 true] [17592186129209 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371107 13194139535862 true] [17592186129209 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371114 13194139535862 true] [17592186129224 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371051 13194139535862 true] [17592186129224 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371058 13194139535862 true] [17592186129224 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371065 13194139535862 true] [17592186129224 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371100 13194139535862 true] [17592186129225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371072 13194139535862 true] [17592186129225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371079 13194139535862 true] [17592186129225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371086 13194139535862 true] [17592186129225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371093 13194139535862 true] [17592186129482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126698 13194139535862 false] [17592186129482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370990 13194139535862 true] [17592186129482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535862 true] [17592186129482 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le y\\le 2},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 true] [17592186129482 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{4}{5}kg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): - 1 \\le x \\le 1,-2 \\le y \\le 2}$,\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{2}m$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 false] [17592186129482 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 4=8\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{4}{5})}{8}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{-2+2}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-2}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{16}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{40}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535862 true] [17592186129484 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126710 13194139535862 false] [17592186129484 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370996 13194139535862 true] [17592186129484 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535862 true] [17592186129484 1100 "A circular plate of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $2\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $4\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 true] [17592186129484 1100 "A circular plate of mass $2kg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $2m$ centered at the origin.\n\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $4m$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535862 false] [17592186129484 1145 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{2}{4\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{